   "id": "yoma.85a.5",
   "daf": "85a",
   "sentenceId": 4,
   "he": "מִי שֶׁנָּפְלָה עָלָיו מַפּוֹלֶת וְכוּ׳. מַאי קָאָמַר?",
   "en": "§ It was taught in the mishna: With regard to «one upon whom a rockslide fell,» and there is uncertainty whether he is there under the debris or whether he is not there; and there is uncertainty whether he is still alive or whether he is dead; and there is uncertainty whether the person under the debris is a gentile or whether he is Jew, one clears the pile from atop him. The Gemara asks: «What is» the mishna «saying?» Why does it bring three different uncertainties to illustrate the principle that one violates Shabbat to save a life even in a case of uncertainty?"
  }
 ],
